# Campus Turkey — environment
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file is not, and must never
# acquire a real value. Every variable below is validated at boot by server/lib/config.ts
# — the process refuses to start rather than failing on the first request that needed one.
#
# Legend:  [required]  always  ·  [prod]  required in production only  ·  [optional]


# ── Runtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# development | test | production. Set by the platform in deployed environments.
NODE_ENV=development


# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [required] Pooled connection, used by every request. On Neon or Supabase this is the
# URL with `-pooler` in the host. Serverless functions open a connection per invocation
# and a direct connection exhausts Postgres within minutes of real traffic.
#
# Do NOT add `pgbouncer=true` on Neon. It tells Prisma to stop using named prepared
# statements, which costs several extra round trips on every query. Measured against a
# Neon endpoint in one session: bare pooled 229 ms/query, with `pgbouncer=true` 1496
# ms/query — 6.5x, and 10x once `connection_limit=1` is added on top. Neon's pooler has
# kept named prepared statements across checkouts since 2024, so the flag protects
# against a problem this provider no longer has. Verified with 240 statements across 8
# concurrent pooled sessions.
#
# On a pooler that genuinely cannot do this — plain PgBouncer in transaction mode, and
# Supabase's port 6543 — the flag IS required, and the cost above is the price of
# correctness there. Check your provider rather than copying either answer.
#
# `connect_timeout` matters on a platform whose compute suspends when idle: the first
# connection has to wake it, and the 5s default expires mid-wake and reports P1001
# "can't reach database server", which reads like a firewall problem and is not one.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host-pooler.region.provider.tech/campus_turkey?sslmode=require&connection_limit=10&connect_timeout=30

# [required] Direct (unpooled) connection. Used by `prisma migrate` only — migrations
# need a session-level connection that a transaction pooler cannot provide.
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host.region.provider.tech/campus_turkey?sslmode=require&connect_timeout=30

# ── Build-time database access ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The university pages are statically generated, and there are 40 of them in 17
# languages. Next renders those 680 pages across several worker processes at once, so
# the build is briefly the most concurrent database client this system has — far more so
# than serving traffic, where each request does one or two queries and leaves.
#
# With the runtime `connection_limit=10` the build exhausts the pool and fails partway
# through with "Timed out fetching a new connection from the connection pool". That is
# not a symptom of a slow database; it is more render workers than connections.
#
# Raise the limit for the build only, by exporting a different DATABASE_URL in the build
# command. On Vercel that is a build-time environment variable; locally:
#
#   DATABASE_URL="...&connection_limit=30&pool_timeout=30" npm run build
#
# Do NOT raise the runtime limit to match. A serverless runtime opens a pool per
# instance, and a high per-instance limit multiplied by the instance count is how a
# provider's connection ceiling is reached during a traffic spike.


# ── Site ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [required] Public origin, no trailing slash. Canonical URLs, the sitemap, Open Graph
# tags, the cookie domain and the CSRF origin check all derive from this. Must be https
# in production — the session cookie is Secure-only.
#
# The production value is https://campusturkey.org. It must be present in the *build*
# environment, not only at runtime: `next build` renders the sitemap, the canonicals and
# every `hreflang` pair, so a value supplied only to the running server arrives after the
# URLs have already been written. Nothing warns you — the pages simply carry the wrong
# origin. Same failure mode as NEXT_PUBLIC_MAPTILER_KEY further down.
SITE_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000


# ── Authentication ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [required] At least 32 characters. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Rotating this signs every session out, which is the intended behaviour in an incident.
SESSION_SECRET=

# ── Rate limiting ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [prod] Upstash Redis. Required in production: this deploys to a serverless runtime
# where an in-process counter enforces the limit divided by the instance count, which
# is no limit at all. Free tier is sufficient at this traffic.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=


# ── Bot protection ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [prod] turnstile | hcaptcha | disabled. Required in production — handoff note 13.
# The public lead forms collect passport-adjacent and, on the medical desk, health data.
#
# The implemented provider is hCaptcha: `features/leads/captcha.tsx` renders the widget
# and `leads.service.ts` verifies the token. `turnstile` remains a valid value for the
# server's verification call, but no Turnstile widget exists — selecting it would
# produce no token and refuse every submission.
CAPTCHA_PROVIDER=disabled

# The PRIVATE half. Server only; never prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#   hCaptcha dashboard → Settings → Secret key
CAPTCHA_SECRET=

# The PUBLIC half, rendered into the widget. Both are required and neither substitutes
# for the other — without the secret the server cannot verify, and without this the
# browser produces no token to verify.
#   hCaptcha dashboard → Sites → your site → Site key
#
# Inlined at BUILD time like every NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable, so it must be set for the
# build as well as at runtime. Without it, `CAPTCHA_PROVIDER=hcaptcha` refuses to boot.
NEXT_PUBLIC_HCAPTCHA_SITE_KEY=


# ── Translation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [optional] deepl | google | azure | disabled. When disabled, /api/translate returns
# 503 and the client stays in English — the designed degradation, not a failure.
#
# The key is server-side only. Never prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_, or it ships in the
# browser bundle and becomes someone else's translation budget.
TRANSLATE_PROVIDER=disabled
TRANSLATE_API_KEY=
TRANSLATE_ENDPOINT=https://api-free.deepl.com/v2/translate


# ── Payouts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [optional] wise | airwallex | flutterwave | unconfigured.
# Blocked on client open question 3 (which provider). While unconfigured, the
# payout-method endpoints return 422 with an explanation rather than pretending to work.
#
# PAYOUT_WEBHOOK_SECRET is not optional once a provider is set: an unverified webhook
# endpoint is an unauthenticated write endpoint that can mark a withdrawal as paid.
# It signs `${timestamp}.${body}` with HMAC-SHA256; point the provider's callback at
# POST /api/webhooks/payouts and configure it to send `x-signature` and `x-timestamp`.
PAYOUT_PROVIDER=unconfigured
PAYOUT_API_BASE=
PAYOUT_API_KEY=
PAYOUT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=


# ── Transactional mail ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [optional] resend | postmark | disabled. Transactional only — no marketing sends.
MAIL_PROVIDER=disabled
MAIL_API_KEY=
# A bare address, not "Campus Turkey <no-reply@campusturkey.org>". The display-name form
# is what most people write here and what most providers accept, and it is refused at
# boot by `z.string().email()` — with the message "MAIL_FROM: Invalid email", which does
# not obviously mean "remove the name". Set the display name in the provider's dashboard.
MAIL_FROM=


# ── Scheduled jobs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [prod] Bearer token for /api/cron/purge-leads, which hard-deletes leads past their
# retention date. Vercel Cron sends this automatically once the variable is set; the
# route compares it in constant time and refuses everything else.
#
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# [prod] Required in production: the process refuses to boot without it. Without it the
# endpoint returns 503 and the purge never runs, which means personal data is retained
# past the window the consent notice promised — a silent compliance failure rather than
# an outage, and nothing anywhere reports it.
CRON_SECRET=


# ── Document storage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [prod] s3 | local | unconfigured.
#
# `local` writes to `.uploads/` and exists so the upload and review flows can be built
# and tested before a bucket exists. It is REFUSED in production: object storage on a
# serverless platform's local disk is a file that vanishes on the next cold start, and
# finding that out after a student has uploaded their passport is not recoverable.
#
# `s3` speaks to Amazon S3, Cloudflare R2 and Supabase Storage alike — all three
# implement the same protocol. The signing is built in; there is no SDK to install.
STORAGE_PROVIDER=local

# The bucket must be PRIVATE with no public read policy. Nothing in this system ever
# hands out a public object URL: every read is a per-request presigned link that expires
# in five minutes. A bucket left public would make all of that decorative.
S3_BUCKET=
S3_REGION=
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Full origin including scheme.
#   AWS         https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com
#   Cloudflare  https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#   Supabase    https://<project>.supabase.co/storage/v1/s3
S3_ENDPOINT=

# true for Cloudflare R2 and Supabase Storage, false for Amazon S3.
# Getting this wrong signs a different resource than the one requested, and the failure
# is a 403 that reads like a credentials problem.
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=false

# CORS on the bucket must allow PUT from SITE_ORIGIN, or uploads fail in the browser
# while succeeding from curl. Allowed headers: content-type. Exposed headers: etag.


# ── Observability ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [prod] Required in production. Without it, caught boundary errors go unrecorded and
# the first report of an outage is a partner emailing about their payout.
SENTRY_DSN=

# fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace
LOG_LEVEL=info


# ── Maintenance ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# on | off. When on, middleware.ts returns 503 with Retry-After from the edge, before
# the app bundle is involved — handoff note 12. /api/health stays reachable so the
# platform can tell "deliberately down" from "fell over".
MAINTENANCE_MODE=off
MAINTENANCE_RETRY_AFTER_SECONDS=600


# ── Search engines ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# on | off. `off` sends `X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow` on every page, which keeps a
# deployment out of Google entirely while leaving it fully viewable by anyone with the
# link. That is what the client-preview deployment wants: visible to the people you send
# it to, invisible to search.
#
# **Set this to `off` on the staging/preview project. Never set it on production.** The
# default is `on` because this site's entire purpose is organic search, so a variable
# somebody forgot must not be able to deindex the real thing. Only the exact string
# `off` disables indexing; anything else means on.
#
# Deliberately NOT done with a `robots.txt` block. Disallowing a path stops a crawler
# fetching it, which means it never reads the `noindex` and the URL can still appear in
# results as a bare address with no description. Allowing the fetch and refusing the
# index is the combination that actually works, so robots.txt stays permissive.
SEARCH_INDEXING=on


# ── Map tiles ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [prod] MapTiler, for the university directory's Leaflet map. Handoff note 13:
# OpenStreetMap asks that production traffic not use its public tile server.
#
# Style in use: dataviz-light, raster PNG. The full request shape is
#   https://api.maptiler.com/maps/dataviz-light/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=...
# built in src/features/map/tiles.ts, which is also where the style is changed.
#
# THIS KEY IS PUBLIC. Leaflet fetches tiles from the browser, so `NEXT_PUBLIC_` is
# correct and the value appears in the network tab of every visitor. That is normal for
# tile keys and is not a leak — but it means secrecy protects nothing, and the actual
# control is the domain restriction:
#
#   MapTiler Cloud → Keys → your key → Allowed origins → add your production origin
#
# An unrestricted key is someone else's tile bill. Restrict it before launch.
#
# Next inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_* at BUILD time, so this must be set in the build
# environment as well as the runtime one. Present at runtime only gives a server that
# boots cleanly and a browser bundle containing `undefined`.
#
# Without it, development falls back to OpenStreetMap so the page still runs locally.
# Production refuses to boot rather than inherit that fallback.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAPTILER_KEY=

# [optional] An ADDITIONAL host for the CSP img-src allowlist. MapTiler is always
# allowed; this only widens the list, for a self-hosted or proxied tile set. Setting it
# to the MapTiler origin is harmless but redundant.
#   MAP_TILE_HOST=https://api.maptiler.com
MAP_TILE_HOST=
